  {
   "id": "sudoku_sat",
   "title": "Sudoku as SAT",
   "prompt": "<p>Encode Sudoku as CNF, as in the notes. A grid is a list of 9 rows of 9 integers, with 0 for a blank. Variable <code>81*r + 9*c + d</code>, for row and column <code>r, c</code> in 0–8 and digit <code>d</code> in 1–9, means \"cell (r, c) holds d\" (so the variables are 1 to 729).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>var(r, c, d)</code> returns that number.</li>\n<li><code>sudoku_cnf(grid)</code> returns the clauses saying that each cell holds exactly one digit, and each row, each column and each 3 × 3 box holds each digit exactly once, using the notes' <code>exactly_one</code> (one clause listing the group, plus a clause [−<em>a</em>, −<em>b</em>] for every pair), followed by one unit clause [<code>var(r, c, d)</code>] for each given digit, in row-major order.</li>\n<li><code>decode(assignment)</code> turns a satisfying assignment (a dict from variable to bool) back into a 9 × 9 grid.</li>\n</ul>",
   "starter": "def var(r, c, d):\n    pass\n\ndef sudoku_cnf(grid):\n    pass\n\ndef decode(assignment):\n    pass\n",
   "hint": "<p>Build the groups of 9 variables first: 81 cell groups, then for each digit its 9 row groups, 9 column groups and 9 box groups (box cells are <code>(3*br + i, 3*bc + j)</code>). Then turn each group into clauses with <code>exactly_one</code>. The count for an empty grid is 4 × 81 × (1 + 36) = 11,988.</p>",
   "solution": "from itertools import combinations\n\ndef var(r, c, d):\n    return 81 * r + 9 * c + d\n\ndef exactly_one(lits):\n    return [list(lits)] + [[-a, -b] for a, b in combinations(lits, 2)]\n\ndef sudoku_cnf(grid):\n    groups = []\n    for r in range(9):\n        for c in range(9):\n            groups.append([var(r, c, d) for d in range(1, 10)])\n    for d in range(1, 10):\n        for i in range(9):\n            groups.append([var(i, c, d) for c in range(9)])\n            groups.append([var(r, i, d) for r in range(9)])\n        for br in range(3):\n            for bc in range(3):\n                groups.append([var(3 * br + i, 3 * bc + j, d) for i in range(3) for j in range(3)])\n    clauses = [cl for g in groups for cl in exactly_one(g)]\n    for r in range(9):\n        for c in range(9):\n            if grid[r][c]:\n                clauses.append([var(r, c, grid[r][c])])\n    return clauses\n\ndef decode(assignment):\n    grid = [[0] * 9 for _ in range(9)]\n    for r in range(9):\n        for c in range(9):\n            for d in range(1, 10):\n                if assignment.get(var(r, c, d)):\n                    grid[r][c] = d\n    return grid\n",
   "tests": [
    {
     "label": "Variable numbers",
     "call": "[var(0, 0, 1), var(0, 0, 9), var(0, 1, 1), var(1, 0, 1), var(8, 8, 9)]",
     "expect": "[1, 9, 10, 82, 729]"
    },
    {
     "label": "Clause count",
     "type": "assert",
     "code": "empty = [[0] * 9 for _ in range(9)]\ncl = sudoku_cnf(empty)\nassert len(cl) == 11988, len(cl)\nassert sum(len(c) == 9 for c in cl) == 324\ng = [row[:] for row in empty]; g[0][0] = 5; g[4][7] = 2\ncl2 = sudoku_cnf(g)\nassert len(cl2) == 11990 and cl2[-2:] == [[5], [var(4, 7, 2)]], cl2[-2:]\n"
    },
    {
     "label": "A solved grid satisfies the clauses; a broken one doesn't",
     "type": "assert",
     "code": "SOL = [[5,3,4,6,7,8,9,1,2],[6,7,2,1,9,5,3,4,8],[1,9,8,3,4,2,5,6,7],[8,5,9,7,6,1,4,2,3],[4,2,6,8,5,3,7,9,1],\n       [7,1,3,9,2,4,8,5,6],[9,6,1,5,3,7,2,8,4],[2,8,7,4,1,9,6,3,5],[3,4,5,2,8,6,1,7,9]]\ndef as_assign(g):\n    return {81 * r + 9 * c + d: g[r][c] == d for r in range(9) for c in range(9) for d in range(1, 10)}\nsat = lambda cl, a: all(any(a[abs(l)] == (l > 0) for l in c) for c in cl)\npuzzle = [[v if (r * 9 + c) % 3 == 0 else 0 for c, v in enumerate(row)] for r, row in enumerate(SOL)]\ncl = sudoku_cnf(puzzle)\nassert sat(cl, as_assign(SOL))\nbad = [row[:] for row in SOL]; bad[0][1], bad[0][2] = bad[0][2], bad[0][1]\nassert not sat(cl, as_assign(bad)), \"swapping two cells breaks a column, so some clause must fail\"\nassert decode(as_assign(SOL)) == SOL\n"
    },
    {
     "label": "Every row, column and box constraint is there",
     "type": "assert",
     "hidden": true,
     "code": "empty = [[0] * 9 for _ in range(9)]\ncl = {tuple(sorted(c)) for c in sudoku_cnf(empty)}\nv = lambda r, c, d: 81 * r + 9 * c + d\nassert tuple(sorted([-v(0, 0, 4), -v(2, 2, 4)])) in cl, \"box rule missing\"\nassert tuple(sorted([-v(3, 1, 7), -v(8, 1, 7)])) in cl, \"column rule missing\"\nassert tuple(sorted([-v(5, 0, 2), -v(5, 8, 2)])) in cl, \"row rule missing\"\nassert tuple(sorted([-v(4, 4, 1), -v(4, 4, 9)])) in cl, \"cell rule missing\"\nassert tuple(sorted([-v(0, 0, 4), -v(3, 3, 4)])) not in cl, \"cells in different boxes, rows and columns shouldn't conflict\"\n"
    }
   ]
  }
